ulations, we teased out a higher number of specific DBD-DNA hydrogen bonds in\n                  <jats:italic>P. cineraria,</jats:italic>\n                  leading to a stronger DNA-binding affinity compared to drought-sensitive\n                  <jats:italic>Arabidopsis thaliana</jats:italic>\n                  . These results directly support copy number expansion of AP2/ERF transcription factors and the evolution of their structures for more efficient DNA search and binding as drought adaptation mechanisms in\n                  <jats:italic>P. cineraria</jats:italic>\n                  .\n                </jats:p>",
